articles.content_en:
Article 3 Where a person obligated to enforce a judgment or ruling of a People's Court falls under any of the following circumstances, the act of refusing to enforce the judgment or ruling shall be deemed as "causing serious circumstances":
(1) After the People's Court issues a notice of enforcement, concealing, transferring, selling, or destroying property that has been sealed up, distrained, or inventoried and placed under the person's custody by order, or transferring property that has been frozen, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(2) Concealing, transferring, selling, or destroying property provided as security to the People's Court during enforcement, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(3) Obstructing or resisting enforcement by violence or threat, thereby making enforcement work impossible to carry out;
(4) Gathering a crowd to create a disturbance, storming the enforcement scene, besieging, detaining, or beating enforcement personnel, thereby making enforcement work impossible to carry out;
(5) Destroying or seizing enforcement case materials, enforcement service vehicles, other enforcement equipment, enforcement personnel uniforms, or enforcement service documents, causing serious consequences;
(6) Other acts of obstructing or resisting enforcement that cause serious consequences.
